In the context of Judicial Activism, match
List-I (Judicial Doctrine) with
List-II (Application).
List-I:
a. Doctrine of Public Interest Litigation (PIL);
b. Doctrine of Writ jurisdiction;
c. Doctrine of proportionality;
d. Doctrine of separation of powers
List-II: i. Allowed courts to issue writs only for the enforcement of fundamental rights and correcting injustices; ii. Ensured that government actions are balanced and designed to prevent any one branch of government from becoming too powerful and abusing its authority; iii. Maintained the independence of the judiciary by ensuring that a separation of powers among the legislative, executive, and judicial branches; iv. Any member of the public having 'sufficient interest' can approach the court for enforcing the rights of other persons and redressal of a common grievance

In the context of Judicial Activism, match
List-I (Cases) with
List-II (Descriptions).
List-I:
a. Public Interest Litigation (PIL);
b. The Kesavananda Bharati case;
c. The Vishaka guidelines;
d. Justice Puttaswamy vs. Union of India case
List-II: i. Introduced the concept of the Basic Structure of the Indian Constitution, which established that certain fundamental features of the Constitution are immutable and cannot be amended by the Parliament; ii. Right to privacy is protected as an intrinsic part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21 and as a part of the freedoms guaranteed by Part III of the Constitution; iii. A legal action initiated in a court of law for the enforcement of public interest or general interest in which the public or a class of the community have pecuniary interest or some interest by which their legal rights or liabilities are affected; iv. Laid down a set of procedural guidelines for use in India in cases of sexual harassment

